# jemdoc: menu{MENU}{POST.html}, nofooter, nodefaultcss, addcss{../jemdoc.css}, addcss{post}, addjs{post}
= Post Title

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="postmeta"
     data-topic="Federated Unlearning"
     data-date="August 26, 2026"
     data-lang="en"
     data-alt="POST-zh.html"></div>
~~~

# =============================================================================
# HOW THIS TEMPLATE WORKS
#
# One post = two files, one per language.  Never mix languages in one file.
#     POST.jemdoc      data-lang="en"   data-alt="POST-zh.html"
#     POST-zh.jemdoc   data-lang="zh"   data-alt="POST.html"
# The header bar gets a back link to the blog and an EN / 中文 switch built
# from those two attributes.  Drop data-alt and the switch disappears.
#
# post.js does the rest automatically:
#   - numbers ==  and === headings as 1, 1.1, 1.2 ...
#   - builds the "In this post" box from those headings (2 or more needed)
#   - numbers figure captions
#   - numbers footnote marks and links them to the list at the bottom
#   - adds the "Back to all posts" link at the foot
#
# Build it from docs/blog/ :
#     python ..\..\jemdoc -c ..\mysite.conf POST.jemdoc
#
# Everything below is a live example.  Delete what you do not use.
# =============================================================================

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="epigraph">
"A quote worth opening with, if there is one."
<span class="by">— Someone</span>
</div>
~~~

An opening paragraph or two before the first heading, setting up what the post
is about and why it is worth the reader's time. Body text is justified on a
capped measure, the way the book sets its chapters.

== First section

Ordinary paragraphs. Links look like
[https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.04243 this], /italics like this/, *bold like this*.

{{<span class="lead">A run-in lead.</span>}} Open a paragraph with one of
these when it argues a single point --- the book uses them heavily. The rest
of the paragraph continues in normal weight.

=== A subsection

Subsections number themselves 1.1, 1.2 and show up indented in the box at
the top.

== Boxes

Theorem-like blocks are raw HTML. Three flavours, all with a bold run-in head:

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="defn"><span class="head">Definition 1 (Federated unlearning).</span>
Given a model trained over a set of clients, remove the influence of one
client's data as if it had never taken part.
</div>

<div class="thm"><span class="head">Theorem 1.</span>
Under assumptions A and B, the retrained and unlearned models agree to within
\(\epsilon\).
</div>

<div class="note"><span class="head">Note.</span>
An aside that is worth setting apart but is not a formal statement.
</div>
~~~

== Math

Inline math in ordinary text: $\|w_t - w_t^\star\|_2 \le \epsilon$ --- jemdoc
rewrites those dollars into MathJax delimiters for you. It does not touch raw
blocks, so inside one use MathJax's own delimiters instead: backslash-paren
for inline, backslash-bracket for display, as in the theorem box above.

Display math goes in a raw block so jemdoc leaves the backslashes alone:

~~~
{}{raw}
\[
  \min_{\theta}\; \sum_{k=1}^{K} p_k\, F_k(\theta)
  \quad\text{subject to}\quad \mathcal{I}(D_j;\theta) = 0 .
\]
~~~

== Figures

~~~
{}{raw}
<figure>
  <img src="images/POST/fig1.png" alt="what the figure shows">
  <figcaption>What the reader should take from it. Do not write the
  number --- post.js prefixes "Figure 1:" in document order.</figcaption>
</figure>
~~~

Put the images in {{<code>docs/blog/images/POST/</code>}}.

== Footnotes

Mark them inline with a raw span{{<sup class="fn"></sup>}} and they are
numbered in reading order. A second one here{{<sup class="fn"></sup>}}.

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="footnotes"><ol>
<li>The first note. Numbering and the anchor are wired up by post.js.</li>
<li>The second note.</li>
</ol></div>
~~~

== References

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="refs"><ol>
<li>A. Author, "Title of the paper," <i>Venue</i>, 2026.
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0000.00000">arXiv</a></li>
</ol></div>
~~~
